From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 19:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Subtitles: have the AI provider correct what an OCR misread

The repair undoes only the misreadings a subtitle repeats: one font and
one OCR engine make the same mistakes hundreds of times, so a confusion
the file shows often is safe to undo, and one it shows once is not. A
"wouId" that appears once, a name the OCR broke on its only line, stay.
A language model reads them the way a person does, from the sentence.

"Repair with the AI provider", under Repair, asks the provider of
"Translate subtitles" either about the words the rules doubt - no word
of the language, not a name, and either no word's spelling ("R0zer") or
turned into a word by a confusion the file does not show often enough to
be its own - or about every line, for a language no pack is installed
for. The cues go a few to a request, with the lines before them, the
title and the characters' names, and the model answers with a list of
changes, "3: wouId -> would", never a rewritten line.

A change is made only when the rules allow it: what is written stands in
the line as whole words and is no word of the language, and what it
becomes is what the OCR's usual confusions explain ("l" for "I", "rn" for
"m", "0" for "O") - slang a dictionary lacks too, "couIda" is "coulda" -
or the word that pieces which are none were broken from, rejoined. Only
where no such reading is a word may it restore an accent the OCR lost or
misread, into a word of the dictionary or a name of the cast; a letter
more or less is another word ("Berck" is no "Berk"). A name of the cast
is never changed, nor what sits inside a formatting tag. With no
dictionary for the language, only what can be no word's spelling
("wouId", "R0zer") changes: "modern" might as well be "modem", for all
the confusions say. In the doubted-words mode it must be a word the
rules doubted. A subtitle that is right must never come out wrong: a
model that would rather read "Hip" than "Help", or say "went" for "go",
changes nothing.

Over 34 English tracks, whole, the doubted words made 44 changes and every
line, over the first 200 cues of 25, 14: of those 58, 55 were right ("Ancl"
is "And", "N0" is "No", "I'II" is "I'll") and three were not - a town
made a word, a name made another, a word rejoined into the wrong one -
which the rule on accents now refuses, at the cost of three right ones.
Clean tracks came out unchanged. Gemma 4 E4B, served by llama.cpp on
the network.

A track inside the video sends every cue through the provider's thread
once this is on - those it is not asked about too, so they keep their
order and their timing repair - and each reaches libass as soon as its
batch is answered. A file beside the video is known whole, so it is
corrected from where playback is, ahead of it - the parser reads the
repaired file once more, cue by cue, for the doubted words. A provider
that does not answer leaves the subtitle as the rules left it, and the
cues after it go by unasked for a minute rather than wait on it; a seek
is no failure. A subtitle being translated is not corrected first: the
model reads through the damage.

The cue work the translation started now carries notes with each cue, the
doubted words here. COcrEngine::Doubtful() names a repaired line's
doubted words, and CCueCleaner::Clean() hands them out.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
